nodecode-triplescan is a rewrite of dafka007's dafka007/hermes-security-audit:

  https://github.com/dafka007/hermes-security-audit
  commit 60a7661bbe2f255c507d620275c59782cabe0335
  MIT License, Copyright (c) 2026 dafka007 (LICENSE)

The rewrite is the plugin's one model-facing tool, `security_audit`, and its
pre-tool-call approval hook, as a Nodecode cell. What it keeps: the three
scanner invocations -- `gitleaks dir WORKSPACE --report-format json
--report-path - --redact=100 --no-color`, `osv-scanner scan source -r
WORKSPACE --format json`, and `semgrep scan --config CONFIG --json --quiet
WORKSPACE` -- launched without a shell from the folder being audited; the same
reading of their output (Gitleaks' JSON array, OSV's vulnerabilities found
wherever they nest, Semgrep's results together with its analysis errors); the
same statuses and the same precedence (FAIL, then UNRESOLVED, then PASS, with
NOT_APPLICABLE for OSV-Scanner's exit 128 and BLOCKED for a folder that cannot
be read); the same coverage_incomplete rule; the same cap of 200 findings per
scanner and the same 10 MiB output ceiling; the same rule that a secret's value
is never copied out (Gitleaks runs redacted and only the rule, file, line and
fingerprint are read from its report); and the same approval gate, which here is
one question on the tool-call hook.

What is left out: the plugin's child-environment scrubbing, which stripped
Hermes Desktop's Python and terminal variables before each scanner -- a Nodecode
cell has none to strip -- and its HSA_* environment variables, whose settings
now live in the cell's own config section instead of the environment. The
approval question is behind `ask` rather than unconditional as it was in Hermes,
because a cell that always asked would refuse every scan in a shell where
nobody is there to answer; with it on, a call with no operator waiting is
refused exactly as the plugin's prompt would leave the call unanswered. The
plugin's README, its Windows notes and its test suite are not carried over; the
cell's own tests stub the scanners instead.

Nothing of the original's Python is bundled. The cell runs the same three
programs the plugin ran, on PATH, and its own code is Common Lisp.
